WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.985 --> 00:00:06.360
GEOFFREY NOER:
Hello, and welcome

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:08.730
to Introduction and Best
Practices for Google Cloud

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:09.630
Storage.

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:11.940
My name is Geoffrey
Noer, a product manager

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:13.260
for Cloud Storage.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:16.379
And with me today is Lavina
Jain, software engineer

00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:19.212
for cloud storage.

00:00:19.212 --> 00:00:20.670
Today, we'll be
walking through you

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:24.050
through a brief
introduction, including

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:27.230
some information about how
to take advantage of location

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:29.210
types and storage classes.

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:31.100
Then we'll focus
on best practices

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:34.910
for two of our most important
use cases-- content serving,

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:36.470
and compute and analytics.

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:38.660
And then, finally,
some words of wisdom

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:40.550
on performance and
scaling and how to take

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:42.590
advantage of the performance
that cloud storage

00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:45.270
has to offer.

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:47.090
So first of all, what
is cloud storage?

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:50.960
So, not to be confused with
file storage or block storage,

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:53.240
cloud storage is
our object store

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:55.340
and is often by
considered by many

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:59.900
to be the native format
for storage in the cloud.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:01.850
What we provide is
a simple interface

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:06.080
with a consistent API,
strongly consistent listings,

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:09.980
and low latency and equivalent
speed across all of our storage

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:13.280
classes, which makes for
a very smooth experience

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:14.450
on the product.

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:16.490
Reliability is of
utmost importance,

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:19.460
both from a durability as
well as from an availability

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:20.840
standpoint.

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:23.120
Cost effectiveness,
of course, as you

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.070
don't want storage to be
the majority of your budget.

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:30.210
And then, finally, security,
because, of course,

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:32.420
you want to make sure
that you can control

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:34.790
who has access to your
data, and to make sure

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:36.620
that your data is
always encrypted

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:37.760
at rest and in transit.

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:43.570
The foundation, in many
respects, of cloud storage

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:46.180
is a high-performance,
high-quality network

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:48.580
that Google built
for its own purposes

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:50.950
initially for all of
the consumer properties

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:52.760
that you are used to using.

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:57.850
And so this involves multiple
undersea cable investments,

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:01.280
large numbers of points of
presence across the planet,

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:04.870
and as well as a substantial
number of cloud regions.

00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:06.430
And this allows
you to really pick

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:09.639
a location that makes sense for
your data with the knowledge

00:02:09.639 --> 00:02:11.620
that you can
distribute your content

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:14.200
to any part of the globe
that you may need to.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:19.500
The three primary
use cases that we see

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:21.520
are content storage
and delivery.

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:23.280
So for example,
National Geographic

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:27.310
uses cloud storage for its
content serving purposes.

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:32.310
And this is ideal because of
low latency and the ability

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:34.710
to take advantage of
all of those edge caches

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:37.540
to push content
across the globe.

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:40.420
We have a lot of use for compute
analytics and machine learning.

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:44.070
So this is more the high
performance inside the cloud

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:45.800
use of storage.

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:48.270
Here, customers like
the Broad Institute

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:50.700
doing high-performance
genomic sequencing,

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:56.250
analysis, or for that matter,
Twitter doing analytics

00:02:56.250 --> 00:03:00.210
on the overall
corpus of tweets are

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:04.390
both examples of customers using
us for that sort of purpose.

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:06.540
And here, the sky's the limit.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:08.640
You can scale up to
terabits per second

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:11.130
of throughput from a
cloud storage bucket

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:13.560
to make those workloads stream.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:18.210
And then, finally, we have
backup and archiving use cases

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:21.640
where it's really more about
storing the data long term.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:24.150
And so regulatory compliance.

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:25.860
Tools like Bucket
Lock that allow

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:29.580
you to guarantee that data will
not be deleted for a specified

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:30.960
length of time.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:34.200
Those sorts of facilities
really help the use case

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.060
for those needs.

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:41.840
So let's turn first
to storage classes

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:44.050
to go a little bit
deeper into cloud storage

00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:47.570
because it's really about the
locations that you choose,

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:49.000
and then the
storage classes that

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:52.780
determine your
frequency of access

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:57.100
and, ultimately, the
price point that you pay.

00:03:57.100 --> 00:03:59.310
So the first stage
in storing data

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:01.920
is actually to decide what
kind of a location type

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:03.460
you want to use.

00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:05.850
Regions, multi-regions
and dual-regions

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:08.760
are all available
to you with a number

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:12.090
of different specific
location choices within each.

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:14.220
So regions are the
most basic choice.

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:17.010
They allow you to
have all of your data

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:19.149
in one specific region.

00:04:19.149 --> 00:04:22.380
So for example, you might
choose US Central One,

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:26.100
or you might choose
Europe West One.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:28.470
And once you've chosen
that specific location

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:31.180
for your bucket, all of your
data will be stored there.

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:35.370
So the advantage is you can
then run your VMs or other cloud

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:40.380
services and have that compute
and storage be co-located

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:42.570
for the best
possible performance.

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:46.710
And regions also provide a
very attractive price point.

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:49.650
Now, if you want georedundancy
for a higher availability

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:53.582
protecting you against
particular regional outages,

00:04:53.582 --> 00:04:56.040
then what you can do is you
can choose either a dual-region

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:57.900
or multi-region.

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:00.180
Dual-regions are
more like regions

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:03.120
in that you know exactly
where your data is located.

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:06.330
And, namely, they'll be located
in the two specific regions

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:08.590
that are part of
that dual-region.

00:05:08.590 --> 00:05:11.430
And so, again, for analytics
and other workloads

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:13.620
that are high
performance, dual-regions

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:15.480
are usually the right choice.

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:18.150
And then multi-regions,
on the other hand,

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:20.050
you just know that
your data is redundant

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:22.270
and stored somewhere
within a continent.

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:24.870
And this is an ideal
choice for content serving.

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:29.150
So how do we place the data?

00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:32.830
So in a region, which
is the yellow squares,

00:05:32.830 --> 00:05:36.040
if you chose Region B as
your region, all of your data

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:38.740
will be placed in Region B. So
that's the most straightforward

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:40.030
example.

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:42.070
In blue is the dual-region.

00:05:42.070 --> 00:05:44.320
And with the dual-region,
it's one copy

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:46.240
in one region, one
copy and the other.

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:49.670
So your data is redundant across
those two specific regions.

00:05:49.670 --> 00:05:52.360
And then with
multi-regions, every object

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:54.920
might be replicated differently.

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:56.710
And so for your
entire bucket, you're

00:05:56.710 --> 00:06:00.040
likely to have data distributed
across the continent.

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:03.790
And if you're serving
to the internet,

00:06:03.790 --> 00:06:05.830
any additional
latency is not really

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:09.250
a consideration because
of the internet latencies

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:12.730
that will be added to it.

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:15.240
So once you've picked a
particular type of location

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:18.750
type, the next choice has to
do with your default storage

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:19.620
class.

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:21.240
And in reality, you
can have objects

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:24.040
that are in all of
your storage classes.

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:25.740
So you can start
out in standard,

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:30.355
and then also have colder data
that is in nearline, coldline,

00:06:30.355 --> 00:06:30.855
or archive.

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:37.640
So what is the difference
between the storage classes?

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:40.190
It really comes
down to a trade-off

00:06:40.190 --> 00:06:44.430
between price and frequency of
access and length of storage.

00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:46.860
And so if we look at
the best practices,

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:49.190
this is where that
becomes clear.

00:06:49.190 --> 00:06:52.010
If you're going to retain data
for, for example, more than a

00:06:52.010 --> 00:06:55.520
year and only access
it once a year or two,

00:06:55.520 --> 00:07:00.200
that would be an ideal use case
for the archive storage class.

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:01.940
And, in fact, much
of the data there

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:04.190
is intended never to be read.

00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:06.410
Maybe it would only
be read if there

00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:08.870
is a regulatory
compliance issue that

00:07:08.870 --> 00:07:10.550
needs to be looked
into four years

00:07:10.550 --> 00:07:13.732
after some logs are
written, for example.

00:07:13.732 --> 00:07:15.190
But on the other
hand, maybe you're

00:07:15.190 --> 00:07:19.150
doing a really frequently
accessed analytics workload

00:07:19.150 --> 00:07:22.090
where you're churning through
a very large amount of data

00:07:22.090 --> 00:07:23.170
quickly.

00:07:23.170 --> 00:07:26.320
Maybe your data is only
living for a few days,

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:30.290
but other objects are
staying around for longer.

00:07:30.290 --> 00:07:31.968
That's where standard
comes into play.

00:07:31.968 --> 00:07:34.510
And then, of course, nearline
and coldline are in the middle.

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:38.680
So nearline is usually
for once a quarter

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:44.260
and retained for
at least one month.

00:07:44.260 --> 00:07:46.030
Whereas coldline is
at least retained

00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:48.700
for at least three
months, and you

00:07:48.700 --> 00:07:51.500
don't want to access it
only a few times a year.

00:07:51.500 --> 00:07:54.760
So you can use this
as a good benchmark.

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:56.600
But so then what do you do?

00:07:56.600 --> 00:08:00.400
Well, this is where you take
advantage of object lifecycle

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:01.630
management.

00:08:01.630 --> 00:08:05.410
And we provide all of the
utilities inside the product

00:08:05.410 --> 00:08:07.120
to be able to
automatically migrate

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:09.820
your data from standard
down to nearline

00:08:09.820 --> 00:08:11.813
to coldline to archive.

00:08:11.813 --> 00:08:14.230
And you can do that based on
the age of the objects, which

00:08:14.230 --> 00:08:16.660
is usually very
applicable to workloads

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:20.050
because cold data is usually
infrequently accessed.

00:08:20.050 --> 00:08:22.310
You can also manually
move a particular data

00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:27.830
sets with a storage rewrite
to change the storage classes.

00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:30.400
So no matter how
you approach it you

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:33.880
can use the multiple
storage classes

00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:36.669
as a way of optimizing your
total cost of ownership

00:08:36.669 --> 00:08:38.799
so that your cold
data is charged

00:08:38.799 --> 00:08:42.309
the least, and your hot data
the most, as you would hope for.

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:47.500
Security is critical, and
we provide a good amount

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:49.690
of flexibility in the product.

00:08:49.690 --> 00:08:52.150
So one thing that you'd
never need to worry about

00:08:52.150 --> 00:08:54.160
is whether your
data is encrypted.

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:57.120
So your data is always encrypted
at rest and in transit,

00:08:57.120 --> 00:08:59.210
and that's not even an option.

00:08:59.210 --> 00:09:02.043
So some other services
just make it--

00:09:02.043 --> 00:09:04.210
you have to be a little bit
more careful about that.

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:06.610
With us, it's always encrypted.

00:09:06.610 --> 00:09:09.160
The default is that the
keys for the encryption

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:12.260
are actually stored in
Google and managed by Google.

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:15.220
And then, depending on your
level of paranoia and desire

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:17.860
for control over
the encryption, you

00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:19.480
can take more and
more control, first

00:09:19.480 --> 00:09:22.660
by still keeping the keys
inside the key management

00:09:22.660 --> 00:09:24.940
service inside Google
Cloud, but where

00:09:24.940 --> 00:09:29.020
you manage the key
rotations and revocations.

00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:33.070
So that's kind of a best of
both worlds, in some respects.

00:09:33.070 --> 00:09:36.100
And then, if you want
full, complete control,

00:09:36.100 --> 00:09:38.830
you can actually supply
the keys with every access

00:09:38.830 --> 00:09:42.790
and have a key server running
on premises outside of Google

00:09:42.790 --> 00:09:45.820
Cloud, in which case Google
really literally could not

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:49.900
access your data, even
if you asked Google to.

00:09:49.900 --> 00:09:54.520
With the other two where Google
does have access to the keys,

00:09:54.520 --> 00:09:56.590
any accesses are fully logged.

00:09:56.590 --> 00:10:00.910
And we never look at data
unless there is a support case

00:10:00.910 --> 00:10:04.240
or other legitimate
reason where we

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:05.830
do need to go in
and access the data.

00:10:05.830 --> 00:10:09.880
And again, that would all be
fully logged and transparent.

00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:13.420
So we take security and privacy
both extremely seriously.

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:19.960
So with that basic
introduction to the framework

00:10:19.960 --> 00:10:23.320
of location types and
storage classes and security,

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:27.550
let's see how those choices
apply to the first of our two

00:10:27.550 --> 00:10:30.350
use cases that we're going to
discuss today, which is content

00:10:30.350 --> 00:10:30.850
serving.

00:10:33.580 --> 00:10:35.970
So there are really four
primary considerations

00:10:35.970 --> 00:10:38.730
with content serving.

00:10:38.730 --> 00:10:41.220
Some people decide
to do direct serving.

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:44.490
Others use the Google Cloud
CDN product as a complement

00:10:44.490 --> 00:10:46.800
to cloud storage.

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:50.320
Others combine cloud storage
with a custom front-end,

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:53.190
which also might be
used with Cloud CDN.

00:10:53.190 --> 00:10:55.080
And then, finally,
there's the opportunity

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:57.930
to use independent
CDNs outside of Google

00:10:57.930 --> 00:11:02.460
to combine with cloud
storage to actually stream

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:05.350
or serve the data from those
independent third parties.

00:11:07.908 --> 00:11:09.950
So for direct serving,
this is, in many respects,

00:11:09.950 --> 00:11:11.690
the easiest approach.

00:11:11.690 --> 00:11:13.712
It's fully integrated
with the product.

00:11:13.712 --> 00:11:15.420
There's nothing more
that you need to do.

00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:17.900
You can do this just straight
out of the cloud storage

00:11:17.900 --> 00:11:18.830
offering.

00:11:18.830 --> 00:11:22.160
And here there's
some real advantages

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:24.620
in that we automatically
use the premium Google

00:11:24.620 --> 00:11:28.100
network for distribution
and take advantage

00:11:28.100 --> 00:11:30.740
of the edge caching
if you turn it

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:32.730
on with cache control headers.

00:11:32.730 --> 00:11:35.490
So go ahead and turn the
cache control headers on.

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:37.640
And that will allow
repeated accesses

00:11:37.640 --> 00:11:40.400
to have significantly lower
latency than, of course,

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:42.830
the first cold access
would have had.

00:11:42.830 --> 00:11:45.860
So it's, in many ways, a
built-in content distribution

00:11:45.860 --> 00:11:47.540
network.

00:11:47.540 --> 00:11:51.020
In terms of the location
type, we would typically

00:11:51.020 --> 00:11:53.540
advise using multi-regions
because this provides you

00:11:53.540 --> 00:11:57.500
with the highest possible
availability and basically

00:11:57.500 --> 00:12:00.080
continent-based serving.

00:12:00.080 --> 00:12:02.840
And that actually can
even be serving out,

00:12:02.840 --> 00:12:05.570
for example, a US
multi-region to the globe.

00:12:05.570 --> 00:12:09.440
Or you might actually have a
different multi-region buckets

00:12:09.440 --> 00:12:12.730
in different continents to
serve those populations.

00:12:12.730 --> 00:12:14.300
It really depends
on your use case

00:12:14.300 --> 00:12:17.405
and how your
content is oriented.

00:12:20.410 --> 00:12:24.150
What Cloud CDN does is it
means that all of your reads

00:12:24.150 --> 00:12:28.560
don't go back and hit the
Cloud Storage Service.

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:32.400
Only the cold misses
where you haven't recently

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:35.550
access to that storage does
it go back to cloud storage.

00:12:35.550 --> 00:12:40.290
So you won't have any egress
charges from cloud storage.

00:12:40.290 --> 00:12:43.230
Those will all get handled
by Cloud CDN instead.

00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:45.930
It also provides you
with custom domains

00:12:45.930 --> 00:12:52.390
with SSL, some more flexibility
on the other URLs for access.

00:12:52.390 --> 00:12:53.970
But, of course, the
number one reason

00:12:53.970 --> 00:12:56.160
is to access the
differentiated pricing that's

00:12:56.160 --> 00:12:57.885
available through Cloud CDN.

00:13:02.090 --> 00:13:03.710
Custom front-ends
are a way that you

00:13:03.710 --> 00:13:06.680
can have an intermediary
service between cloud

00:13:06.680 --> 00:13:09.820
storage and the customer.

00:13:09.820 --> 00:13:11.780
This can be popular,
for example,

00:13:11.780 --> 00:13:13.870
if you want to have
an authorization

00:13:13.870 --> 00:13:15.610
stage before access.

00:13:15.610 --> 00:13:18.700
Or if you need to dynamically
transform the data in some way

00:13:18.700 --> 00:13:23.590
before it streams
out from the service.

00:13:23.590 --> 00:13:27.048
So here it depends on whether--

00:13:27.048 --> 00:13:28.340
you might want to use a region.

00:13:28.340 --> 00:13:29.950
You might want to
use a dual-region

00:13:29.950 --> 00:13:33.100
to co-locate the storage
with your custom front-end,

00:13:33.100 --> 00:13:36.340
depending on how much
disaster recovery and business

00:13:36.340 --> 00:13:38.500
continuity you want to
build into the model.

00:13:38.500 --> 00:13:40.870
The dual-region would
be the better choice

00:13:40.870 --> 00:13:42.730
for high availability.

00:13:42.730 --> 00:13:44.560
But if your service
doesn't require that,

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:46.520
a region would be sufficient.

00:13:46.520 --> 00:13:50.910
And then you can still
combine this with Cloud CDN

00:13:50.910 --> 00:13:53.380
to take advantage of the
caching and the other advantages

00:13:53.380 --> 00:13:56.480
that I just talked about.

00:13:56.480 --> 00:13:58.760
If you're using an
independent third party CDN,

00:13:58.760 --> 00:14:02.420
this is, again, an example where
using a region or a dual-region

00:14:02.420 --> 00:14:04.710
makes more sense
than a multi-region.

00:14:04.710 --> 00:14:07.010
And this is because
the whole purpose

00:14:07.010 --> 00:14:10.070
of using an independent
CDN is to have an origin

00:14:10.070 --> 00:14:14.450
shield between the two that
means that only cold content

00:14:14.450 --> 00:14:16.670
hits will end up
going back to Google.

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:20.360
But you want those to be
hitting the Google region that

00:14:20.360 --> 00:14:24.620
is in close proximity to
where the independent CDN has

00:14:24.620 --> 00:14:25.880
their setup.

00:14:25.880 --> 00:14:30.500
So if they're located very
close to Iowa, for example,

00:14:30.500 --> 00:14:32.330
in a data center
near there, you might

00:14:32.330 --> 00:14:36.200
want to use US Central One,
which is the Google region that

00:14:36.200 --> 00:14:39.380
is located in that same state.

00:14:39.380 --> 00:14:43.040
So trying to have a close
proximity and low latency

00:14:43.040 --> 00:14:45.710
between the independent
CDN and Google

00:14:45.710 --> 00:14:48.830
is the first matter
of importance

00:14:48.830 --> 00:14:51.680
for the best performance
with an independent CDN.

00:14:54.470 --> 00:14:57.110
So with that, I'd like to
turn the presentation over

00:14:57.110 --> 00:15:00.005
to Lavina Jain, who
will take it from here.

00:15:00.005 --> 00:15:01.130
LAVINA JAIN: Thanks, Geoff.

00:15:01.130 --> 00:15:03.380
So I'll talk about how to
pick the right location

00:15:03.380 --> 00:15:06.020
date for compute and
analytics workloads,

00:15:06.020 --> 00:15:10.080
and then share some tips on
performance and scalability.

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:13.530
Compute and analytics pipelines
have several types of data,

00:15:13.530 --> 00:15:15.480
and your choice of
location type really

00:15:15.480 --> 00:15:17.840
depends on the type of data.

00:15:17.840 --> 00:15:20.130
First, you have the source data.

00:15:20.130 --> 00:15:22.550
This is data that is
persistent and that you

00:15:22.550 --> 00:15:25.760
need to load up right when
you stock your pipeline.

00:15:25.760 --> 00:15:29.400
What is really important
here is very high throughput.

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:32.390
So you would want to co-locate
your compute with storage,

00:15:32.390 --> 00:15:34.930
and using a region or
a dual-region location

00:15:34.930 --> 00:15:37.170
makes sense.

00:15:37.170 --> 00:15:40.230
The second type of data
is intermediate data.

00:15:40.230 --> 00:15:43.340
This is data that is produced
by one stage of your pipeline

00:15:43.340 --> 00:15:45.870
and consumed by another stage.

00:15:45.870 --> 00:15:49.290
Now, if this data is persistent,
then using cloud storage

00:15:49.290 --> 00:15:51.500
makes sense, and the
same recommendations

00:15:51.500 --> 00:15:53.720
of co-locating
compute with storage

00:15:53.720 --> 00:15:57.180
and using a region or a
dual-region location apply.

00:15:57.180 --> 00:15:59.840
However, many times
this data is very short

00:15:59.840 --> 00:16:03.800
lived and users need high
throughput and low latency.

00:16:03.800 --> 00:16:05.510
In that case,
actually, cloud storage

00:16:05.510 --> 00:16:09.260
is not the best product that
you could use for this data.

00:16:09.260 --> 00:16:14.680
Consider using local SSD or
persistent disk, for example.

00:16:14.680 --> 00:16:18.730
The third type of data is
side inputs and staging data.

00:16:18.730 --> 00:16:22.510
This data is typically read
only once per workload.

00:16:22.510 --> 00:16:25.060
So choice of location
type doesn't really

00:16:25.060 --> 00:16:29.480
matter much here because cloud
storage does a lot of caching.

00:16:29.480 --> 00:16:32.890
So even if the false
read is remote and slow,

00:16:32.890 --> 00:16:36.330
the success of
reads will be fast.

00:16:36.330 --> 00:16:38.640
And finally, you
have final outputs

00:16:38.640 --> 00:16:40.710
that your pipeline produces.

00:16:40.710 --> 00:16:44.190
The choice of location type
and storage class for this data

00:16:44.190 --> 00:16:48.160
really depends on what you
plan to do with the data.

00:16:48.160 --> 00:16:51.540
For example, if your pipeline
is producing processed images

00:16:51.540 --> 00:16:55.110
to be sold to internet users,
then really the recommendations

00:16:55.110 --> 00:16:58.560
that Jeff shared earlier about
content-serving workloads

00:16:58.560 --> 00:16:59.730
apply.

00:16:59.730 --> 00:17:03.960
Or if these final outputs are
fed into another pipeline,

00:17:03.960 --> 00:17:07.140
then it really acts as source
data, and the recommendations

00:17:07.140 --> 00:17:12.210
that I shared earlier
about source data apply.

00:17:12.210 --> 00:17:14.819
So before moving on, I want
to give a quick shout-out

00:17:14.819 --> 00:17:16.619
to Cloud DataProc Service.

00:17:16.619 --> 00:17:19.619
There has been a lot of interest
in moving Hadoop workloads

00:17:19.619 --> 00:17:22.380
into cloud, and Cloud
DataProc Service

00:17:22.380 --> 00:17:24.339
lets you do exactly that.

00:17:24.339 --> 00:17:27.030
It is a managed Hadoop
service that can bring up

00:17:27.030 --> 00:17:29.460
new clusters really fast.

00:17:29.460 --> 00:17:32.490
There is also a connector
for Cloud Storage that

00:17:32.490 --> 00:17:35.100
lets you read your
data from Cloud Storage

00:17:35.100 --> 00:17:39.720
directly without having to
first copy it into HDFS.

00:17:39.720 --> 00:17:43.360
There are a few gotchas
to be aware of here.

00:17:43.360 --> 00:17:47.100
First one is that Cloud
Storage is not a file system.

00:17:47.100 --> 00:17:49.740
We do not have
directory semantics.

00:17:49.740 --> 00:17:53.160
So you have to get around that
by eliminating any dependencies

00:17:53.160 --> 00:17:55.750
on [INAUDIBLE]
directory operations.

00:17:55.750 --> 00:17:58.800
For example, you could
upload your data temporarily

00:17:58.800 --> 00:18:00.420
in a temporary directory.

00:18:00.420 --> 00:18:04.740
And then, when the data set is
complete, rename the directory.

00:18:04.740 --> 00:18:07.810
Or you could load directly
into the final directory.

00:18:07.810 --> 00:18:11.100
But when you're done with data,
write a sentinel file that

00:18:11.100 --> 00:18:13.890
says, hey, I'm done with data.

00:18:13.890 --> 00:18:19.710
Secondly, cloud storage
latency is typically higher

00:18:19.710 --> 00:18:24.100
compared to HDFS, especially
for small objects.

00:18:24.100 --> 00:18:27.840
So you could get around that by
parallelizing small operations

00:18:27.840 --> 00:18:30.360
or using larger object sizes.

00:18:30.360 --> 00:18:33.550
And third, as I mentioned
before for temporary data,

00:18:33.550 --> 00:18:35.070
consider using local SSD.

00:18:37.620 --> 00:18:41.100
Now let's talk about
performance and scaling.

00:18:41.100 --> 00:18:44.470
So first, let's understand
the general performance

00:18:44.470 --> 00:18:47.670
characteristics
of object storage.

00:18:47.670 --> 00:18:49.650
One of the things
to remember here

00:18:49.650 --> 00:18:52.380
is that latency
for small objects

00:18:52.380 --> 00:18:54.240
could be relatively high.

00:18:54.240 --> 00:18:57.720
At 95th percentile,
a 1-byte read

00:18:57.720 --> 00:18:59.790
could take about
100 milliseconds.

00:18:59.790 --> 00:19:02.510
And the same applies
for write as well.

00:19:02.510 --> 00:19:06.330
The trends of cloud storage are
really horizontal scalability

00:19:06.330 --> 00:19:08.650
and single-screen throughput.

00:19:08.650 --> 00:19:10.560
So for a large
enough object, you

00:19:10.560 --> 00:19:14.520
should be able to get about 800
megabits per second for read

00:19:14.520 --> 00:19:18.730
and 400 megabits per
second for writes.

00:19:18.730 --> 00:19:20.230
What this means
is you would want

00:19:20.230 --> 00:19:25.060
to favor large objects sizes
and avoid unnecessary sharding.

00:19:25.060 --> 00:19:27.700
Now, even if you are able
to pack most of your data

00:19:27.700 --> 00:19:29.950
into large objects,
you may still

00:19:29.950 --> 00:19:32.780
have those internal
segments or structured data,

00:19:32.780 --> 00:19:36.700
for example, that typically
tend to be smaller in size.

00:19:36.700 --> 00:19:39.580
See if you can tune those
up to be a bit larger.

00:19:39.580 --> 00:19:42.210
At least 2 megabytes is
the recommended size.

00:19:42.210 --> 00:19:44.770
Larger is, of course, better.

00:19:44.770 --> 00:19:47.770
And then, if you have to
use small object sizes,

00:19:47.770 --> 00:19:50.050
then parallelize your
requests to get it

00:19:50.050 --> 00:19:54.410
on the inherent
latency of the system.

00:19:54.410 --> 00:19:57.230
Now let's talk about how to
get the best performance out

00:19:57.230 --> 00:19:58.520
of your writes.

00:19:58.520 --> 00:20:00.710
There are three different
write strategies

00:20:00.710 --> 00:20:02.390
that you can choose from.

00:20:02.390 --> 00:20:05.860
The simplest one is called
non-resumable upload,

00:20:05.860 --> 00:20:10.730
where you can upload your entire
object using a single request.

00:20:10.730 --> 00:20:12.680
The second one is
called resumable upload,

00:20:12.680 --> 00:20:16.340
where you first send a request
to start a resumable upload

00:20:16.340 --> 00:20:19.910
session, and then send
subsequent requests to upload

00:20:19.910 --> 00:20:22.880
data using the same session ID.

00:20:22.880 --> 00:20:25.300
Now, if one of the
upload requests fail,

00:20:25.300 --> 00:20:28.780
that you can query the
server to find the last

00:20:28.780 --> 00:20:31.840
offset that it could
successfully commit, and then

00:20:31.840 --> 00:20:34.720
resume writing from that offset.

00:20:34.720 --> 00:20:37.580
The third approach is
quite parallel upload,

00:20:37.580 --> 00:20:40.360
where you split your
object into multiple parts,

00:20:40.360 --> 00:20:44.290
upload those parts in
parallel, and then compose.

00:20:44.290 --> 00:20:47.200
Compose is mostly a
metadata-only operation

00:20:47.200 --> 00:20:50.500
and does not involve
rewriting bytes.

00:20:50.500 --> 00:20:55.180
I say mostly because it
does cause data deletion.

00:20:55.180 --> 00:20:57.700
And then, depending on
certain options that you pick,

00:20:57.700 --> 00:21:00.640
it may result in rewriting data.

00:21:00.640 --> 00:21:03.890
So be careful with the
options that you pick there.

00:21:03.890 --> 00:21:08.140
So the recommendation here is
to use non-resumable upload

00:21:08.140 --> 00:21:10.630
by default. The
bar to switch over

00:21:10.630 --> 00:21:13.630
to start using presumable
upload or parallel upload

00:21:13.630 --> 00:21:15.190
is really high.

00:21:15.190 --> 00:21:16.900
That's because
resumable upload has

00:21:16.900 --> 00:21:20.230
a drawback that uploading
every single object

00:21:20.230 --> 00:21:22.590
takes a minimum of 2 requests.

00:21:22.590 --> 00:21:24.910
Now, if the first request
to start the session

00:21:24.910 --> 00:21:27.040
takes about 100
milliseconds, that

00:21:27.040 --> 00:21:29.890
could be a really large
part of your upload

00:21:29.890 --> 00:21:32.330
time for small objects.

00:21:32.330 --> 00:21:34.660
So the rule of thumb
here is to multiply

00:21:34.660 --> 00:21:37.720
your expected throughput
by 30 seconds.

00:21:37.720 --> 00:21:40.760
And that gives you the cutoff
in terms of object sizes

00:21:40.760 --> 00:21:44.920
when it makes sense to switch
over to using resumable upload.

00:21:44.920 --> 00:21:47.950
To give you some concrete
numbers, if you have clients

00:21:47.950 --> 00:21:51.670
uploading using cable at
[? 4 ?] Mbps upload speed,

00:21:51.670 --> 00:21:54.970
then the cutoff object
size is 15 megabytes.

00:21:54.970 --> 00:22:00.040
Or if you have clients
over VM getting a full 400

00:22:00.040 --> 00:22:03.430
Mbps us upload speed,
then you shouldn't really

00:22:03.430 --> 00:22:05.590
have to use a resumable
upload unless you

00:22:05.590 --> 00:22:10.280
have objects as large as 1
and 1/2 gigabytes or larger.

00:22:10.280 --> 00:22:13.820
The bar to switch to parallel
upload is even higher.

00:22:13.820 --> 00:22:17.030
You shouldn't need it unless
you're uploading using a VM

00:22:17.030 --> 00:22:20.560
and need more than 400
megabits per second.

00:22:20.560 --> 00:22:24.010
I do want to call
out a gotcha here,

00:22:24.010 --> 00:22:29.800
that compose really makes
sense in standard storage class

00:22:29.800 --> 00:22:33.400
only because it could
trigger early deletions.

00:22:36.300 --> 00:22:39.990
Lastly, I want to talk about
scaling and hotspotting.

00:22:39.990 --> 00:22:42.570
Cloud Storage generally
scales really well,

00:22:42.570 --> 00:22:44.910
and you don't have to worry
about object [INAUDIBLE]

00:22:44.910 --> 00:22:47.910
and object sizes, though
there's one case where

00:22:47.910 --> 00:22:50.940
we have seen users run into
some scaling bottlenecks,

00:22:50.940 --> 00:22:53.620
and that's localized
hotspotting.

00:22:53.620 --> 00:22:55.600
To explain this,
let me first explain

00:22:55.600 --> 00:22:58.780
what happens under the hood,
and then go over recommendations

00:22:58.780 --> 00:23:00.950
on how to get around it.

00:23:00.950 --> 00:23:03.840
Cloud Storage uses
a sharding strategy

00:23:03.840 --> 00:23:06.000
called Range-Based Sharding.

00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:09.130
So we store your objects
across multiple servers.

00:23:09.130 --> 00:23:11.480
And the way we distribute
your objects to servers

00:23:11.480 --> 00:23:15.980
is by splitting the object
namespace into ranges.

00:23:15.980 --> 00:23:18.650
In this example
here, any objects

00:23:18.650 --> 00:23:21.290
that begin with a
letter between E and G

00:23:21.290 --> 00:23:23.240
will go into the fourth shard.

00:23:23.240 --> 00:23:26.310
Objects beginning with
a letter between G and S

00:23:26.310 --> 00:23:29.140
will go into the second
shard, and so on.

00:23:29.140 --> 00:23:32.720
What this allows us to do is
provide consistent listing.

00:23:32.720 --> 00:23:36.380
So consider a user request to
list all objects that start

00:23:36.380 --> 00:23:38.150
with D/.

00:23:38.150 --> 00:23:41.150
This request will be
routed to the third shard,

00:23:41.150 --> 00:23:44.120
and looking up this one
shard is enough to find

00:23:44.120 --> 00:23:47.890
all objects starting
with D. Now, consider

00:23:47.890 --> 00:23:50.020
an alternate sharding
strategy, which

00:23:50.020 --> 00:23:52.360
is called hash-based sharding.

00:23:52.360 --> 00:23:54.790
Here the shards are
assigned to servers based

00:23:54.790 --> 00:23:57.220
on the hash of the object name.

00:23:57.220 --> 00:24:00.620
This distributes the
objects all over the place.

00:24:00.620 --> 00:24:02.980
But the same listing
operation would now

00:24:02.980 --> 00:24:06.040
have to look up
every single shard.

00:24:06.040 --> 00:24:08.440
Worse yet, if one of
the shards is slow,

00:24:08.440 --> 00:24:12.530
then the latency of the
entire list operation goes up.

00:24:12.530 --> 00:24:15.350
Even worse scenario is,
imagine what happens

00:24:15.350 --> 00:24:17.310
if one of the shard fails.

00:24:17.310 --> 00:24:19.700
Then we could either
return incomplete results

00:24:19.700 --> 00:24:22.370
or fail the entire
list operation.

00:24:22.370 --> 00:24:24.860
So hash-based sharding
doesn't really

00:24:24.860 --> 00:24:28.190
scale well when we have to
provide consistent listing.

00:24:28.190 --> 00:24:32.600
Hence, we chose to go
with range-based sharding.

00:24:32.600 --> 00:24:35.360
Now, one of the shards
could get very hot,

00:24:35.360 --> 00:24:37.830
and we handle that by
doing auto scaling.

00:24:37.830 --> 00:24:42.520
So we detect hot shards
and split them up.

00:24:42.520 --> 00:24:46.240
So I want to give you a sense of
when does this behavior really

00:24:46.240 --> 00:24:47.830
kick in.

00:24:47.830 --> 00:24:51.700
So you can easily do
up to 1,000 object

00:24:51.700 --> 00:24:54.130
writes per second
on 5,000 object

00:24:54.130 --> 00:24:57.880
reads per second before really
running into this scenario.

00:24:57.880 --> 00:25:01.330
Also, typically, we try to
detect hot shards ahead of time

00:25:01.330 --> 00:25:04.160
and split them up so you
don't notice anything.

00:25:04.160 --> 00:25:08.390
But you can expect a
delay of up to 20 minutes.

00:25:08.390 --> 00:25:11.110
The key thing to
making this work really

00:25:11.110 --> 00:25:15.880
well is really to distribute the
load evenly across key ranges.

00:25:15.880 --> 00:25:19.300
For majority cases, auto
scaling provides the scalability

00:25:19.300 --> 00:25:22.610
that you need without
any other considerations.

00:25:22.610 --> 00:25:25.300
But there is one pattern
that trips this up,

00:25:25.300 --> 00:25:26.815
and that is sequential access.

00:25:29.590 --> 00:25:33.810
So let's talk about some of
the object naming patterns that

00:25:33.810 --> 00:25:35.880
can cause sequential access.

00:25:35.880 --> 00:25:39.340
The first one is using
Azure month and day format.

00:25:39.340 --> 00:25:42.210
The second is generating
order numbers or user IDs

00:25:42.210 --> 00:25:46.260
using monotonically increasing
or sequential numbers.

00:25:46.260 --> 00:25:49.520
And the third one is
using a timestamp.

00:25:49.520 --> 00:25:52.940
So you can get around it by
choosing alternate object

00:25:52.940 --> 00:25:54.350
naming schemes.

00:25:54.350 --> 00:25:57.140
For example, you can pick
a part of your object name

00:25:57.140 --> 00:26:00.140
that is more variable
and bring it up front.

00:26:00.140 --> 00:26:01.890
Log type, in this example.

00:26:01.890 --> 00:26:05.210
So if you're accessing different
kinds of logs on a single day,

00:26:05.210 --> 00:26:07.490
those accesses
would be distributed

00:26:07.490 --> 00:26:09.710
over multiple shards.

00:26:09.710 --> 00:26:12.500
Don't use a sequential
number if you don't have to.

00:26:12.500 --> 00:26:15.540
You could use a UUID instead.

00:26:15.540 --> 00:26:19.080
And the third approach is
to hash the object name

00:26:19.080 --> 00:26:22.020
or part of the object name,
and then prefix the object

00:26:22.020 --> 00:26:23.700
name with the hash.

00:26:23.700 --> 00:26:26.850
The drawback of this approach
is that you cannot do in-order

00:26:26.850 --> 00:26:28.050
listing.

00:26:28.050 --> 00:26:30.660
So if you care about
in-order listing,

00:26:30.660 --> 00:26:33.600
then you could map the
hash to a fixed number.

00:26:33.600 --> 00:26:35.190
10, for example.

00:26:35.190 --> 00:26:38.040
And then, to list an order, you
could do a [INAUDIBLE] look-up

00:26:38.040 --> 00:26:40.740
and merge the results.

00:26:40.740 --> 00:26:43.830
And finally, I want to
add that you don't always

00:26:43.830 --> 00:26:46.530
have to change the
object naming scheme.

00:26:46.530 --> 00:26:49.150
In case of bulk
operations, for example,

00:26:49.150 --> 00:26:51.720
you could simply make sure
that all your accesses are

00:26:51.720 --> 00:26:55.090
distributed randomly and evenly.

00:26:55.090 --> 00:26:58.900
So to summarize, what we
